ious_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 1: you have used 186 actions; a strong score needs about 39 or fewer. You are 4.8x over the target.\nIf you are not steadily making progress toward the level goal, commit to your single best hypothesis and the shortest sequence that tests it \u2014 do not exhaustively scan rows/columns or enumerate every option. Test one idea, read the result, then decide.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "The blue bar stayed at [44,16]-[47,35] even after DOWNx4. So the blue bar CAN move from rows 40-43 to rows 44-47 when aligned with the yellow platform, but it CANNOT move further DOWN from rows 44-47.\nSo the blue bar's range when aligned with the yellow platform is rows 44-47 (and above). It can't reach rows 48-51 or beyond.\nWait, but earlier when the blue bar was NOT aligned with the yellow platform, it was blocked at rows 40-43. When aligned, it can move to rows 44-47. So the yellow platform extends the blue bar's downward range by 4 rows (from 40-43 to 44-47).\nBut the blue bar still can't reach the yellow platform at rows 52-59. There's a gap of 4 rows (48-51) between the blue bar's lowest position (44-47) and the yellow platform (52-59).\nHmm, so the blue bar can get closer to the yellow platform when aligned, but still can't touch it.\nWait, let me reconsider. Maybe the blue bar CAN overlap with the yellow platform. Let me check: the blue bar is at rows 44-47, cols 16-35. The yellow platform is at rows 52-59, cols 16-27. The blue bar's bottom edge is at row 47, and the yellow platform's top edge is at row 52. There's a gap of 4 rows (48, 49, 50, 51) between them.\nSo the blue bar can't reach the yellow platform. But maybe the blue bar CAN move further DOWN if I align it more precisely with the yellow platform?\nCurrently, the blue bar is at cols 16-35, and the yellow platform is at cols 16-27. The blue bar extends 8 cols to the right of the platform (cols 28-35). Maybe the blue bar needs to be fully within the platform's horizontal range (cols 16-27) to move further DOWN?\nBut the blue bar is 20 cols wide, and the platform is 12 cols wide. So the blue bar can't fit entirely within the platform's horizontal range.\nUnless... the blue bar can shrink? But the blue bar is always 80 pixels (4x20). It doesn't shrink.\nOK, so the blue bar can move DOWN to rows 44-47 when aligned with the yellow platform, but no further. This is a new discovery, but I still don't know what the goal is.\nLet me think about what happens when the blue bar is at rows 44-47, cols 16-35. What if I press SPACE at this position? Maybe SPACE does something different when the blue bar is at this position.\nLet me try pressing SPACE when the blue bar is at rows 44-47, cols 16-35.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Blue bar at [44,16]-[47,35], can't move further DOWN\n# Try pressing SPACE at this position - maybe SPACE does something different here\n\nresult = action(['SPACE'])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nfor n in seg['nodes']:\n    b = n['boundary']\n    rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n    cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n    rmin, rmax = min(rows), max(rows)\n    cmin, cmax = min(cols), max(cols)\n    print(f\"  {n['color']}: {n['pixels']}px, [{rmin},{cmin}]-[{rmax},{cmax}]\")\nprint(f\"level_completed: {last_action_result.get('level_completed')}\")\nprint(f\"gameplay_changed: {last_action_result.get('gameplay_changed')}\")\n\n# If SPACE didn't work, try moving the blue bar LEFT/RIGHT while at rows 44-47\nif not last_action_result.get('gameplay_changed'):\n    result = action(['LEFT'])\n    seg = current_frame.segmentation\n    for n in seg['nodes']:\n        if n['color'] == 'b':\n            b = n['boundary']\n            rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n            cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n            print(f\"After LEFT: blue at [{min(rows)},{min(cols)}]-[{max(rows)},{max(cols)}]\")\n    print(f\"gameplay_changed: {last_action_result.get('gameplay_changed')}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "N: 26px, [0,0]-[0,25]\n  W: 38px, [0,26]-[0,63]\n  O: 3508px, [1,0]-[59,63]\n  c: 12px, [1,36]-[3,39]\n  M: 16px, [4,36]-[7,39]\n  b: 80px, [44,16]-[47,35]\n  Y: 80px, [52,16]-[59,27]\n  Y: 80px, [52,40]-[59,51]\n  w: 256px, [60,0]-[63,63]\nlevel_completed: False\ngameplay_changed: False\nAfter LEFT: blue at [44,12]-[47,31]\ngameplay_changed: True", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 4 actions in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: DOWN, DOWN, DOWN, DOWN.\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 187, level 1.\nValid actions right now: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, SPACE, MOUSE.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `last_animation` (per-action frames of your last `action(...)` call) and `frame_stats` (how much this game has animated so far) -- optional; use them only if you judge the intermediate motion informative rather than incidental.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view, and `current_frame.ascii` only for a small specific region; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`,